data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","item_id":"msg_b4c4093aa0144c1e99edf47aec271f7d","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"## Setting Up\n\nSince \\(n\\) is divisible by \\(12 = 2^2 \\cdot 3\\), we know \\(n\\) contains \\(2^2\\) and \\(3^1\\) at minimum.\n\nWe need \\(d(n) = 12\\). The possible factorization shapes giving 12 divisors are:\n- \\(p^{11}\\)\n- \\(p^5 q\\)\n- \\(p^3 q^2\\)\n- \\(p^2 q r\\)\n\n## Testing Candidates\n\n**Form \\(p^2 q r\\):** Take \\(2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot r\\) with \\(r\\) a new prime. Smallest new prime is 5:\n$$n = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 60, \\qquad d(60) = 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 12 ✓$$\n\n**Form \\(p^3 q^2\\):** Need \\(2^2\\) and \\(3\\), so \\(3^2\\) must appear: \\(n = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 72\\) (or \\(2^2\\cdot 3^2 \\cdot\\) nothing smaller works). That's larger than 60.\n\n**Form \\(p^5 q\\):** Would need \\(2^5 \\cdot 3 = 96\\) or \\(3^5 \\cdot 2^2\\) — both larger, and \\(2^5\\cdot 3\\) only gives \\(2^5\\cdot3^1\\) which lacks the required structure at minimum 96.\n\n**Form \\(p^{11}\\):** Impossible (needs only one prime).\n\n## Verifying Nothing Smaller Works\n\nMultiples of 12 below 60:\n- \\(12 = 2^2\\cdot3\\): \\(6\\) divisors\n- \\(24 = 2^3\\cdot 3\\): \\(8\\) divisors\n- \\(36 = 2^2\\cdot3^2\\): \\(9\\) divisors\n- \\(48 = 2^4\\cdot3\\): \\(10\\) divisors\n\n**60**","logprobs":[],"sequence_number":312}

